AUDIO: 'This is Mac OS, a beautiful and convenient operating system for work and creativity. Why does the creative class love Apple devices so much? [music] A consistent interface, speed, reliability, but that\'s not the main'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reason. Look at this. This is Apple Music, a typical Apple interface. Do [music] you like it? Well, it gives you the ability to listen to almost any track ever released. From a functional standpoint, it\'s magnificent. But now'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look at this player. This is Winnab. You can also listen to music on it, but it has something more. [music] There\'s a reason why almost 30 years after its release, even after several redesigns, this design is still remembered. These'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two interfaces don\'t just look different. They represent two fundamentally different world views. If you want to understand a generation, don\'t listen to what its witnesses say. Look at what it creates. This is Windows'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'XP as we remember it from 30 years ago. But beyond this nostalgic image, it was by modern standards a terribly unstable [music] system. And its instability wasn\'t just about errors. You could accidentally break the system'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because [music] its architecture allowed you to remove protection from system folders and delete files essential for its operation. [music] And then there was the zoo of devices that required drivers which would break with every'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system update. By today\'s standards, the entire system was not just terribly designed, but all your work, all your data was under constant threat of being lost. Windows had a problem. It [music] was the most popular operating system in'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the world. The user base was growing. New audience segments were emerging, and the computer was expected to perform more and more functions. Every major update, the so-called service packs tried to include new software and'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] new features. But Microsoft couldn\'t keep up with all the basic needs. For example, Internet Explorer couldn\'t figure out how to [music] invent tabs. So, everything had to be kept in separate windows. And for a long time, Windows Media Player lacked the'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basic function of a media library. So, what did users do? They looked for alternatives. Someone had to solve these problems. And in the case of music, it was Justin Frankle, [music] an American programmer from Arizona, and Dimmitri'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Balderev, a Russian developer who created the first version of Winamp in 1997. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; just two developers. Yet many features we now take for granted were established back then. Drag and drop playlists, an equalizer, visualizations, a modular'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] architecture, and importantly, Frankle himself designed the interface. Frankle was earning tens of thousands of dollars a month. But how if Winamp was free, a modest donate $10 button? And despite the relatively small user'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'population at the time, thousands of users clicked and donated because it was by a huge margin the best way to listen to music on a PC. We\'ve already listed the landmark features that set it apart from the system player and others.'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] But any software is not just about features. It\'s also about the interaction with them. The llama jingle, the famous funny sound at the program\'s launch. &gt;&gt; Win. It really whips the llama\'s ass. And Justin Frankle named his company'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Nullesoft, an ironic jab at Microsoft. These kinds of deeply personal details were everywhere. They weren\'t necessary at all, but Justin was making something he wanted to use himself. Something that wasn\'t good by objective metrics, but'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was cool and fun to him with his own unique authoral vision. And this permeated everything from the features that seemed convenient to the design that mimicked real buttons all in a bright color scheme that he found'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pleasing. This interface doesn\'t look modern to us. Why? Because it has character and style. The modern approach offers a clear narrative. The best interface is the one you don\'t notice. We don\'t associate character with'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'functionality. For [music] us, the opposite is true. And this is the main difference between us and the geek designers of the mid90s. They saw no contradiction between startling functionality, usability, [music]'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and their own character. They even saw benefits in it, and for good reason. Take the design of Windows XP. It replaced the outdated Windows 98 and was completely new at the time. But the trend was towards more character, not minimalism. Yet, in terms of usability,'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'XP was far ahead of 98 as it was in technical specs. And to this day, it looks unique and is recognizable from a distance by its very distinctive elements. All technologies emerge as innovations, but over time, they become'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'familiar and invisibly natural. But true character and uniqueness, even as generations of technology pass, can never become obsolete. [music] It is definitionally timeless. Win amp and XP have left our lives, but even 30 years'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'later, users create simulators and emulators to at the very least bring back the external attributes of that time and I dare say enjoy these unique design solutions. And this philosophy penetrated even the most conservative,'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most corporate corners of the digital [music] world. How should software for documents look? Probably serious without distractions. But this is an assistant, a living paperclip with eyes [music] clippy. Why? He wasn\'t just given the function of an assistant. He was given a'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'personality, an annoying, intrusive one, but a personality nonetheless. Because the creators believed that working with a soulless document could be a little more human. They tried to build a friend inside the most boring program on Earth.'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They failed in terms of usability. But they succeeded in creating something unforgettable. And that captures the entire spirit of that era. Let\'s look at these few interfaces. These are far more functional voice assistants with'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'artificial intelligence. They are infinitely more useful, but they are also infinitely [music] more boring. And this isn\'t just visual. They are by their very nature incredibly bland. It\'s part of their protocol to be neutally supportive. Minimalism and functionalism'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extend to the very character of what we can call intelligence. [music] These are interfaces and designs created by different cultures, at least cultures with fundamentally [music] different valuebased approaches to design. Will this chat GPT interface be'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remembered in the same way we remember Clippy only as a visual backdrop in a conversation about a breakthrough technology? These interfaces are simple and simple [music] is a crucial word for us now. Simple to learn predictable'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interfaces like LLM chats. [music] I think this says more about the modern approach to design than anything else. More than the simplified settings menu in Mac OS 26 or the identical messaging apps. AI is an incredible technology'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that has already transformed our experience with information. A technology that gives us hope we haven\'t seen since the invention of electricity. But is that optimism visible in these interfaces? We see them literally'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everywhere every day, but they have no character. They simply don\'t have the task of conveying that optimism of having their own vibrancy. And what\'s worse, they have the opposite task to be completely invisible and simple. Now, of course, we understand that adding'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character to a design can come at the cost of broad audience reach, and reach, adoption, and loyalty are direct predictors of revenue, and that\'s probably normal. But look at these examples. A single thread runs through'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them all, an attempt [music] to make it human. Sometimes more corporately, reaching out to the user with friendliness, sometimes through an authoral approach, but everywhere there is a desire to make it individual and to evoke an emotion. They could have simply'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not done it, but [music] the geek creators did. Yes, maybe you don\'t find it beautiful, but it is interesting, unique, not boring. And that in itself says an awful lot. Look at the websites, services, and applications [music] we'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use. You are not just looking at visuals. You are looking at manifestations of different perceptions of [music] the world, different beliefs and faiths, beliefs about who we are and how we should think, perceive and exist. All the modern convenient minimalist'

[07:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interfaces with identical elements built [music] on identical design systems. This is the belief that function must lead form. that the best interface is no interface. That we have made a huge leap forward [music] by understanding'

[07:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'affordance and learning to apply it everywhere and that we are so clever for being able to cut away everything superfluous and unnecessary maintaining maximum simplicity and universality. [music] If you try to boil it all down to one'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing, what do you think happened? I\'m sure you thought about functionalism and the growth of the industry. may be about corporations and the datadriven approach or about the importance of technology and [music] features since we\'re talking'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about the design of features in the tech sphere. The truth is Microsoft was already a mega corporation in the early 2000s, but it made the Luna design for Windows XP and the corporation AOL bought Winamp because it was a truly'

[08:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'valuable asset. I\'ll go even further. Dualingo with its awful owl and annoying green color, that\'s also a corporation. Yet, it\'s a modern and vibrant brand with a character that while obnoxious is still a character.'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; No, we clearly see that people are still drawn to personalities, to uniqueness. [music] How much easier it is to promote products through a personal brand than'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to promote the brand itself, especially if it\'s minimalist and safe. There is nothing in the world of money or in the world of technology that makes it impossible for the interface of chat GPT, Notion or Apple Music to have its'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own character. We can make vibrant and unique designs. The main hard to accept fact is that the key change that brought us to where we are is that we as a society no longer need it. We have'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different priorities. But it\'s important to remember that another path exists. All the interfaces around us very clearly and coherently broadcast. We are more efficient, more technological, better and simpler, but at the same time'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terribly [music] boring and devoid of individuality. It\'s great and interesting to look at old software made in the era before the religion of minimalism and functionalism was established.'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The problem is that time has passed [music] and using most of these technologies is simply inconvenient. Why did this particular religion become the common ground for everyone? Is Apple solely to [music] blame? And how is this similar to the story of the invention of'

[09:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standardized shipping [music] containers? We talk about that in another video. You can watch it by clicking the link there. You will understand that I\'m not saying it was good then and bad now. I\'m not trying to contrast [music] the approaches. I\'m'

[10:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
